WHAT DECIDED IT: only codex-luna writes the FILTERED claim list to the artefact.

    render_json(&queued_claims(&claims, &promoted), &contradictions)

claude-sonnet discards the promoted list entirely -- `let (_, verify_rejections) = ...` -- and
glm-53-flash binds it and then renders `&claims` anyway. Both report a verification count over an
artefact that still contains every refused claim, so fb-prove would go on generating tests for
claims the verifier had just refused. The measurement happens, is reported, and does not act.

That is my spec's fault as much as theirs. I wrote "run_cmd calls promote_verified and reports
both counts" and never said the artefact must be filtered. The task's stated purpose -- "so a
false claim cannot become a test" -- requires it. codex-luna inferred the purpose; the other two
followed the letter and are not wrong to have done so.

VERIFIED END TO END, on this morning's real incident. `fb promote liveness-cut`:

    TESTABLE      3
    PROMOTED      1
    REFUSED-BY-VERIFICATION 2

and the claims artefact drops from 3 entries to 1. The two fabricated claims -- the ones quoting
`Cut::Keep { because: "output is too weak to justify ending a run" }`, a string that occurs zero
times in the file they name -- are now refused automatically. That is the whole chain working:
claim-verify into core this morning, verify-wire into fb now, catching the incident that
motivated both.

THE CRITIQUE WAS GOOD, AND BOTH ITS CLAIMS ARE CONFIRMED. codex-luna on glm-53-flash:

  1. `refused_positions.push(local)` stores a batch-LOCAL index, then `refused_positions
     .contains(&pos)` tests it against a GLOBAL index from `members`. A refused claim survives
     whenever its group is not at the start of the list -- a fabricated claim gets promoted by
     the very function written to refuse it. Verified at glm-53-flash promote.rs:811-816.
  2. `verification_line(promoted.len(), refused.len(), ..)` is fed the full rejection vector,
     which `promote_verified` builds by extending core rejections with verification ones. A line
     labelled "refused by verification" counts Incomplete and Duplicate too.

Worth recording against this critic's history: four false-absence claims on port-defects, two
fabricated claims on liveness-cut, a favourable projection on claim-verify, and now two confirmed
findings of real defects. It is not reliably wrong, which is exactly why claims get verified
rather than critics distrusted.

